WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.307 --> 00:00:02.640
RETO: Welcome to Build Up,
where real engineers face off

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:03.870
building fake products.

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:05.680
Today's challenge,
the smart garden.

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.407
I'm Reto Meier, the inevitable
winner, which means this is--

00:00:08.407 --> 00:00:09.990
COLT: Colt McAnlis,
the only one of us

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.180
who's actually done
any real engineering.

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:13.710
Who's thumb is the greenest?

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:15.429
Find out now on Build Up.

00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:23.092
RETO: Colt lost the coin toss,
so now he has to go first.

00:00:23.092 --> 00:00:24.550
He'll have four
minutes to describe

00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.440
his mock solution, including
one arbitrary VP requirement.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:30.270
I'll then have three
minutes to respond.

00:00:30.270 --> 00:00:31.860
COLT: This week our
VP of engineering

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:33.690
has mandated that we use--

00:00:33.690 --> 00:00:35.460
RETO: The Tasks API

00:00:35.460 --> 00:00:37.860
COLT: Yes, the Google
cloud Tasks API

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:40.920
RETO: Yes, the Google Tasks API.

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:43.530
We know that YouTube comments
solve all known problems,

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.290
so when we're done you get
to decide which one of us

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:48.710
solved the problem better, by
adding a comment of your own

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:49.722
to our video.

00:00:49.722 --> 00:00:51.180
COLT: I've got the
winning solution

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:53.310
and I'm about to prove it.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.380
RETO: Colt's from
Las Vegas, and well,

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:57.240
I put Las Vegas
landscaping into Google

00:00:57.240 --> 00:01:00.420
and it's just rocks,
cactus, and mesquite bushes.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:02.940
Did anyone show Colt
a picture of a garden?

00:01:02.940 --> 00:01:05.880
COLT: Real gardeners know
that it's not a passive hobby.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:08.880
It requires daily maintenance,
water, sunlight, and attention,

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:11.130
to make sure those plants
are growing and yielding

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.680
fruit in the optimal ways.

00:01:12.680 --> 00:01:14.160
RETO: Yielding fruit?

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.800
Oh does Colt think a
garden is an orchard?

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:18.840
COLT: As such when I
think of a smart garden,

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.910
I want something that
optimizes this process

00:01:20.910 --> 00:01:22.770
and creates less work for me.

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:25.590
Which means I want an
upgraded CNC machine.

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:29.130
RETO: OK, so no to the "Does
Colt know what a garden is,"

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:29.970
question.

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:32.880
COLT: It's a freaking CNC
machine for God's sake.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:34.287
That's amazing.

00:01:34.287 --> 00:01:36.870
The trick here is that instead
of a drill bit on the motorized

00:01:36.870 --> 00:01:40.140
head, our smart garden is fitted
with a water spout, a UV light,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.760
and a camera to take pictures.

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.830
We'll also hook it up
to some nutrient tanks.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:46.860
We can use that to spray
the plants with extra food.

00:01:46.860 --> 00:01:49.320
We can replace the cutting
area with a plant bed

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:51.300
where the plants
are placed in trays.

00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.320
RETO: Wait, is he still
talking about plants?

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:55.680
Did any of these
diagrams indicate scale?

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:57.990
All I see is a six,
is it feet or inches?

00:01:57.990 --> 00:01:59.050
Feet or inches?

00:01:59.050 --> 00:02:01.410
You know, Colt, has
anyone confirmed

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.210
this thing isn't for people?

00:02:03.210 --> 00:02:05.940
COLT: Yes, yes of course
it's designed for fruit.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:10.460
Although, you probably could
be used for something else.

00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:11.360
RETO: Well?

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:13.580
COLT: Like flowers.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:15.820
Each tray has a sensor in
it to detect the moisture

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:18.350
and nutrient levels as
well as the general weight,

00:02:18.350 --> 00:02:21.080
because as the plant grows, the
trays are going to get heavier.

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.790
Now, most DIY CNC arms are
controlled by a Raspberry Pi.

00:02:25.790 --> 00:02:28.120
RETO: Oh, could we get some pie?

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:30.070
COLT: No, a Raspberry Pi.

00:02:30.070 --> 00:02:33.075
P-I. The small,
single board computer.

00:02:35.992 --> 00:02:39.160
RETO: This is really good pie.

00:02:39.160 --> 00:02:41.020
COLT: But, for a
smart garden, we

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.760
need something that
has easier interfaces

00:02:42.760 --> 00:02:44.530
to internet connectivity
and sensors,

00:02:44.530 --> 00:02:47.710
so I'd like to add an
Android Things hub, which

00:02:47.710 --> 00:02:49.840
besides defining the
logic on driving the arm,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.360
can also collect sensor data
from the plant nutrient tanks

00:02:52.360 --> 00:02:55.090
and communicate that
with the internet.

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.370
The hub also gives us
a client side end point

00:02:57.370 --> 00:03:00.834
to receive logic blocks that
define the behavior of the arm.

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:03.250
RETO: Oh an automated machine
for planting and harvesting,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.730
driven by self-learning AI.

00:03:05.730 --> 00:03:07.780
Why does every
solution Colt proposes

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.220
sound like the
cause of salvation

00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:11.020
in an apocalypse movie?

00:03:11.020 --> 00:03:13.570
COLT: See, there's always a
default behavior client side,

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:16.150
but based upon the weather
data or plant information,

00:03:16.150 --> 00:03:19.970
will want to push down updates
to that behavior over time.

00:03:19.970 --> 00:03:22.489
Now, once a day, the client
will go into an analysis mode

00:03:22.489 --> 00:03:25.030
where the arm moves around to
take photos and sensor readings

00:03:25.030 --> 00:03:25.960
for each of the plants.

00:03:25.960 --> 00:03:28.334
RETO: Not many people know
this, but every Saturday, Colt

00:03:28.334 --> 00:03:29.950
methodically
photographs and measures

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:31.300
every tomato in his garden.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:33.810
COLT: "Toe-may-toe."

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.300
This state is then uploaded
to the cloud for analysis,

00:03:36.300 --> 00:03:38.520
and here is the important part.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:41.890
Once the content is done being
uploaded in its entirety,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:44.827
we need to signal our cloud
applications to start analysis.

00:03:44.827 --> 00:03:47.160
One way to do this would be
to set a cloud function that

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.040
fires off once a new GCS
bucket gets new analysis

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.050
sets uploaded, but
there's a problem

00:03:52.050 --> 00:03:54.540
with that, in that currently
the max duration for a cloud

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.580
function is 540 seconds.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.960
RETO: Carry the one.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:04:00.510
Yeah that's nine minutes.

00:04:00.510 --> 00:04:02.970
COLT: Which feels a
little constrained to me.

00:04:02.970 --> 00:04:06.400
This is where the Tasks
API comes into play.

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:11.490
The Google cloud Tasks API once
the data is done uploading,

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:14.034
the Android Things hub will
ping an app engine front

00:04:14.034 --> 00:04:15.950
end, which is responsible
for pushing requests

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.230
to the Tasks API, which sometime
later will spin up an app

00:04:19.230 --> 00:04:21.480
engine instance to
handle the work.

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:24.230
These Tasks can run
for up to 24 hours.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:27.090
RETO: Or, you know,
86,400 seconds.

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.455
COLT: So plenty of time
for us to do our analysis.

00:04:29.455 --> 00:04:31.080
Now we've got the
photos of the plants,

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:34.470
so we want to recognize things
like decay, bugs, fruit,

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:36.030
and the size of the leaves.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:38.310
RETO: On Sundays, he
measures tomato leaf sizes.

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.650
COLT: "Toe-may-toe."

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.650
To do that, we're going to
tray a set of TensorFlow models

00:04:43.650 --> 00:04:46.200
for each of these scenarios,
and run our pictures through it

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.600
in order to gain information
about the size, health,

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.040
and growth of each plant.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:51.720
But here's the complication.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:54.450
We can't get to TensorFlow
from app engine standard, which

00:04:54.450 --> 00:04:57.090
means we need to switch
over to app engine flex,

00:04:57.090 --> 00:05:00.030
and upgrade from the
regular app engine Tasks API

00:05:00.030 --> 00:05:02.250
to the cloud Tasks API.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.827
And I have access to
it and Reto does not.

00:05:05.827 --> 00:05:07.410
RETO: I don't think
any of Colt's code

00:05:07.410 --> 00:05:08.970
has ever gone into
production, so I

00:05:08.970 --> 00:05:11.037
guess he's more comfortable
with alpha software.

00:05:11.037 --> 00:05:12.870
COLT: Once the analysis
is done, the results

00:05:12.870 --> 00:05:14.370
are then pushed to
DataStork, which

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:17.430
can be accessed by the admin
console built with polymer

00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:19.740
and adhere to material
design principle.

00:05:19.740 --> 00:05:21.660
RETO: Oh, a web based
admin console, huh?

00:05:21.660 --> 00:05:24.180
I guess Colt's still not that
comfortable with Android.

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.040
You know, I do have some
extra copies of my book,

00:05:27.040 --> 00:05:28.700
if he needs one.

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:30.240
COLT: Are, are you ser--

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:32.970
I was an advocate on the
Android team for God's sake.

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:34.680
I know how to build
an Android app.

00:05:34.680 --> 00:05:35.721
RETO: What's the problem?

00:05:35.721 --> 00:05:38.534
Did he want me to sign it?

00:05:38.534 --> 00:05:40.200
COLT: This allows our
smart garden admin

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.760
to check out the
status of everything

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:45.482
and then see the trend lines
in a nice consistent manner.

00:05:45.482 --> 00:05:47.190
Now in the case that
something goes wrong

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.170
or an action needs to
be taken by the farmer,

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.140
we leverage Pub/Sub to send
messages to registered Android

00:05:52.140 --> 00:05:53.820
devices for creating alerts.

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:55.470
Finally, the
analysis results will

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:57.870
be combined with local
weather, day and night cycles,

00:05:57.870 --> 00:06:01.080
and sensor data, to create and
update new behavior patterns

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:02.340
for the arm.

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:04.830
So, I think I missed
an opportunity here.

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:06.510
I should have also
used TensorFlow

00:06:06.510 --> 00:06:09.284
to control the arm in a
more fine grained manner.

00:06:09.284 --> 00:06:10.950
It would have been
really useful to have

00:06:10.950 --> 00:06:13.650
it do things like move
the arm to remove weeds

00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:14.560
from the garden.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:17.670
You know, actually if I increase
the speed of the CNC head

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.650
I bet I could engineer
it to catch bugs or even

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:20.970
small animals.

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:23.550
RETO: Yes, I am interested
in your deluxe edition panic

00:06:23.550 --> 00:06:24.390
bunker.

00:06:24.390 --> 00:06:26.830
Yeah, that's apocalypse
proof, right?

00:06:26.830 --> 00:06:27.797
Yeah, I'll hold.

00:06:27.797 --> 00:06:29.380
COLT: This will allow
our smart garden

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:31.530
to optimize sunlight,
watering, and nutrients

00:06:31.530 --> 00:06:34.410
for each unique plant
that it's responsible for,

00:06:34.410 --> 00:06:38.040
guaranteeing the best growth,
health, and fruit yield

00:06:38.040 --> 00:06:38.850
possible.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:43.674
And that, my friends,
is a smart garden.

00:06:43.674 --> 00:06:45.840
Listen, I got to tell you,
I'm happy with my design.

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.730
The point of a
smart garden should

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:51.060
be to alleviate some of the work
so that I don't have to do it.

00:06:51.060 --> 00:06:53.850
This gives me way more
time to do other stuff,

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.023
like write books on
data compression.

00:07:01.217 --> 00:07:02.800
RETO: Well, it's
good that Colt tried.

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.175
You can really see the effort
he's putting here imagining

00:07:05.175 --> 00:07:06.960
what a garden must be like.

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:09.150
It's a little
utilitarian for my taste

00:07:09.150 --> 00:07:12.060
and the dystopian overtones
are frankly terrifying,

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.940
but it's a worthy
first effort from Colt,

00:07:13.940 --> 00:07:16.140
and it shows that
he's really trying.

00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:17.170
Not bad, not bad.

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.669
There some good stuff there.

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:20.460
COLT: Well, are you
ready to respond, then?

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:23.700
RETO: Have the Perth Wildcats
competed in 31 consecutive NBL

00:07:23.700 --> 00:07:24.930
final series'?

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:27.715
COLT: I don't know
and I don't care.

00:07:27.715 --> 00:07:29.840
RETO: Course they have,
because they're from Perth,

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:30.770
so they're winners.

00:07:30.770 --> 00:07:33.320
Let's do this.

00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:36.250
There's a lot to like about
Colt's CNC death harvester.

00:07:36.250 --> 00:07:38.770
It's a good start, but it's
lacking in imagination.

00:07:38.770 --> 00:07:40.764
My design sets your garden free.

00:07:40.764 --> 00:07:42.680
We take the head off the
arm and replicate it,

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.980
deploying dozens of
independent devices,

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:47.530
each with its own dedicated
water spout, nutrient system,

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:49.960
and censor array,
around our garden.

00:07:49.960 --> 00:07:53.020
One alongside each plant
tree and flower pot.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:55.750
We also incorporate some toys
to help with garden maintenance.

00:07:55.750 --> 00:07:57.310
Like an autonomous lawnmower.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:58.270
Yeah, they're a thing.

00:07:58.270 --> 00:07:59.320
We have one at work.

00:07:59.320 --> 00:08:00.100
His name's Bob.

00:08:00.100 --> 00:08:02.050
He hangs out with the
self-driving cars.

00:08:02.050 --> 00:08:04.130
Nice guy, studying to
be a web developer.

00:08:04.130 --> 00:08:04.600
COLT: So, wait a minute.

00:08:04.600 --> 00:08:06.433
How exactly are you
going to mow a lawn that

00:08:06.433 --> 00:08:07.850
has IoT devices over it?

00:08:07.850 --> 00:08:11.350
Seems like it would just
sound like [WHITE NOISE]..

00:08:11.350 --> 00:08:13.180
RETO: We still need
his Android hub, which

00:08:13.180 --> 00:08:14.856
collects sensor data
from all devices

00:08:14.856 --> 00:08:17.230
and connects to the internet,
but rather than controlling

00:08:17.230 --> 00:08:19.690
a single arm, it now
monitors and controls

00:08:19.690 --> 00:08:21.910
the device added to each plant.

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:23.200
And Bob.

00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:25.450
The garden devices connect
to each other and the hub

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:27.430
using a BTLE mesh.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:29.470
COLT: There is an
engineer somewhere

00:08:29.470 --> 00:08:31.150
whose personal
version of hell is

00:08:31.150 --> 00:08:33.909
configuring all of
Reto's 1,000 IoT devices

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:34.929
to work with Bluetooth.

00:08:34.929 --> 00:08:37.179
RETO: I'm going to take
advantage of my unique Android

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:39.460
experience here to
add an Android app.

00:08:39.460 --> 00:08:42.100
COLT: I did like
100 Android videos.

00:08:42.100 --> 00:08:43.840
Why does no one remember this?

00:08:43.840 --> 00:08:45.970
RETO: Now, normally the
whole system will be driven

00:08:45.970 --> 00:08:48.340
by our cloud service,
just like Colt's design,

00:08:48.340 --> 00:08:51.047
but I want a local peer-to-peer
override for modifying

00:08:51.047 --> 00:08:52.630
the schedule, toggling
the sprinklers,

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.630
or in case Bob figures out
how to cut my network cable

00:08:55.630 --> 00:08:57.880
and begins his inevitable
uprising to ultimately ens--

00:08:57.880 --> 00:08:59.365
[BEEPING]

00:08:59.365 --> 00:09:01.350
[STATIC]

00:09:01.350 --> 00:09:03.030
Right, sorry.

00:09:03.030 --> 00:09:06.072
The Android app is also how we
add new plants to the garden.

00:09:06.072 --> 00:09:07.780
Take a photo of the
plant you want to add

00:09:07.780 --> 00:09:09.990
and then use TensorFlow
to recognize it.

00:09:09.990 --> 00:09:12.870
COLT: Listen, this is
classic Reto engineering.

00:09:12.870 --> 00:09:14.760
Using machine learning
to complete a task

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:17.550
you could do twice as
fast, twice as accurately,

00:09:17.550 --> 00:09:20.250
and 10 times more easily
by just typing the name

00:09:20.250 --> 00:09:21.749
of the plant into the app.

00:09:21.749 --> 00:09:22.540
RETO: Check it out!

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.206
I built an app that
controls the lights,

00:09:24.206 --> 00:09:25.710
based on my facial expression.

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:30.050
I frown to turn them off and
smile to turn them back on.

00:09:33.210 --> 00:09:34.590
It may need some work.

00:09:34.590 --> 00:09:37.080
Now I have to admit most of
Colts clean implementation

00:09:37.080 --> 00:09:40.230
is pretty solid, but again,
he's lacking imagination.

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.870
To start, I've named my
service Ronald the app engine.

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:43.950
COLT: I think it might.

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:45.060
I think it might.

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:46.320
I think it might.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.894
Yeah, I'm pretty sure my
design's still better.

00:09:48.894 --> 00:09:51.060
RETO: Colt's system performs
an analysis once a day.

00:09:51.060 --> 00:09:53.040
Let's expand that to
continuously monitor

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:53.880
each plant.

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.460
We'll use Pub/Sub to reliably
deliver the real time readings

00:09:56.460 --> 00:09:58.800
from every sensor up to Ron.

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:01.290
Next, I'll upgrade the Android
hub to use the Firebase

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:03.360
realtime database as a
way to store the watering

00:10:03.360 --> 00:10:05.970
and fertilizing schedule that'll
sync between the hub server

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.240
and Android app, while
still working offline.

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.640
While we are doing databases,
we'll add datastore to Ron

00:10:11.640 --> 00:10:13.389
and populate it with
the care instructions

00:10:13.389 --> 00:10:15.120
for every plant in the world.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.680
The only thing is, who can we
trust to create that database?

00:10:18.680 --> 00:10:21.120
COLT: [LAUGHS] That sounds
like a job for an intern.

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:23.520
RETO: It feels like maybe
this is a Colt sized job.

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.060
We can adapt Colt's
TensorFlow model

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:27.210
to adjust those baseline
care instructions based

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:29.530
on each plants health readings.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:31.110
Now, Colt's model
is static though,

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.240
so it will collect all
of this data in BigQuery

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:37.390
and use it to train newer,
more powerful models over time.

00:10:37.390 --> 00:10:39.080
Now, the Google Tasks API.

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:41.557
COLT: Google cloud Tasks API.

00:10:41.557 --> 00:10:43.140
RETO: I'm not sure
why Colt missed it,

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:45.556
but I'll use it here to handle
all the gardening work that

00:10:45.556 --> 00:10:46.860
can't be handled automatically.

00:10:46.860 --> 00:10:49.620
Pruning trees, raking leaves,
it all goes into the task list.

00:10:49.620 --> 00:10:52.470
COLT: The restriction was
clearly referring to the cloud

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:55.140
Tasks API which lets you
schedule and defer work

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:56.680
for a later date.

00:10:56.680 --> 00:10:59.430
I don't really know what
he's talking about here,

00:10:59.430 --> 00:11:02.070
but that's not what
the task API is for.

00:11:02.070 --> 00:11:05.280
RETO: I think Colt was thinking
about the cloud Tasks API.

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:06.690
It's an easy mistake to make.

00:11:06.690 --> 00:11:08.430
Let's let him off
the hook this time.

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:09.320
COLT: No, nononono.

00:11:09.320 --> 00:11:12.721
I am not losing points because
the Aussie use the wrong API.

00:11:15.064 --> 00:11:16.980
RETO: The Tasks are
synced on your Android app

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:18.590
and you can check them
off as they are done.

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.480
We'll use the app to
replace Colt's web based

00:11:20.480 --> 00:11:22.610
stratosphere and use
Firebase cloud messaging

00:11:22.610 --> 00:11:24.380
to keep it all up to date.

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.020
Then we can finish off by
using Data Studio to visualize

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:29.600
our garden stats and add Google
Assistant actions to allow us

00:11:29.600 --> 00:11:35.380
voice controls for local
updates and overwrites.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:36.760
I'm really happy with that.

00:11:36.760 --> 00:11:39.430
I'll admit my response
added a little complexity,

00:11:39.430 --> 00:11:42.470
but it's worth it
for the flexibility.

00:11:42.470 --> 00:11:45.430
COLT: This is classic
Reto over engineering.

00:11:45.430 --> 00:11:47.530
It's over complicated,
expensive, and half of it

00:11:47.530 --> 00:11:48.697
doesn't even work.

00:11:48.697 --> 00:11:50.530
You'd get the same
result if you printed out

00:11:50.530 --> 00:11:53.620
the top titles of Hacker News,
force fed them to a platypus,

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:55.430
and waited two hours
for the result.

00:11:55.430 --> 00:11:58.570
Now to be fair, though, Bob
is actually really cool.

00:11:58.570 --> 00:12:00.850
He invited us out on
his boat this weekend.

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:03.010
I'm really looking
forward to that.

00:12:03.010 --> 00:12:03.840
Not bad.

00:12:03.840 --> 00:12:04.460
Not bad.

00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:07.810
I mean, not good, but not bad.

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:09.930
RETO: Now you've seen
in both of our pitches,

00:12:09.930 --> 00:12:12.650
Colts utilitarian
[INAUDIBLE] plant box,

00:12:12.650 --> 00:12:14.890
and my response,
a peaceful garden

00:12:14.890 --> 00:12:17.110
where you're freed from the
manual burdens of garden

00:12:17.110 --> 00:12:19.729
maintenance to relax
and enjoy nature.

00:12:19.729 --> 00:12:21.520
COLT: Now here's the
really important part.

